The SSE client (frontend) is using `fetch` + `response.body.getReader()` to stream the response. The issue is how it handles SSE comment lines (the keepalives).

Looking at the frontend SSE parser:
```javascript
for (const line of lines) {
  if (line.startsWith('event: ')) {
    eventType = line.slice(7).trim();
  } else if (line.startsWith('data: ')) {
    const data = line.slice(6);
    try {
      const parsed = JSON.parse(data);
      handleSSEEvent(eventType, parsed, wrappedCallbacks);
    } catch {
      // Skip malformed JSON
    }
    eventType = '';
  }
}
```

The parser correctly ignores comment lines starting with `:` and only processes `event:` and `data:` prefixes. I'm realizing the SSE format uses double newlines to separate events, with each event containing an optional event type followed by the data payload.

Looking at how the backend yields events like `event: thinking\ndata: {...}\n\n`, the frontend splits by newlines and processes the event type first, then the data, then skips the empty line that marks the event boundary. Keepalive comments also work correctly since they don't match either prefix and get ignored along with the trailing empty line.

Now I need to check the AgentContext to see how the "Thinking..." state is managed.

